The two things everybody says about Kyoto — that it is crowded and that it is beautiful — are both true, and the trick to having more of the second than the first is timing. The famous places are empty at seven and unbearable at eleven. Cycling is how you get to three of them before the crowds arrive at any.

## Renting a bicycle

Rent near your accommodation, not near the station, and rent for the whole stay; a day's rate is not much less than a week's. You will be asked to park only in marked bays — the city tows, briskly — and the bays are everywhere once you know to look for the painted rectangles.

## A morning in the east

Start at Fushimi Inari before eight, walk the lower gates and turn back where the crowd thins, then ride north along the canal to the Philosopher's Path and the small temples along it. Ginkaku-ji, the silver pavilion that was never silvered, is at the top and is the right place to stop for the morning. The whole ride is under fifteen kilometres and almost entirely flat.

## Arashiyama, and whether to bother

The bamboo grove is a hundred metres long and, by nine, a queue. Go at dawn or do not go. The monkey park above it is a proper hill and a proper view, and nobody photographs it.

## Osaka is a train, not a trip

<Leg from="kyoto" to="osaka" />

Fifteen minutes on the special rapid service. Dotonbori for the evening, a return train before the last one, and no need to move hotels — which is why the Osaka stop on the map sits inside a Kyoto day rather than starting a new one.

## Hiroshima, if you have the day

<Leg from="kyoto" to="hiroshima" />

An hour and forty minutes on the Shinkansen each way. It is a long day and a serious one; the museum asks for more time than the timetable gives it, and the island of Miyajima, a ferry away, asks for the rest. If the trip has the day, this is what it is for.
